//! `cp-proctex`: procedural textures for the Cubeplanet engine.
//!
//! Responsibility: procedural 16 px PBR textures generated by code, the engine's own,
//! release-safe art.
//!
//! Binding contract: `engine/docs/ARCHITEKTUR.md`, section 4.3.
//!
//! ```
//! use cp_proctex::{Params, average_color, generate, mip_chain, recipe_info};
//!
//! // Defaults: the recipe's own colours.
//! let stone = generate("stone", &Params::new(), 7, 16)?;
//! assert_eq!(stone.albedo.len(), 16 * 16);
//!
//! // Colours and variants per species: a birch log.
//! let birch = Params::new()
//!     .with_color("bark", [226, 222, 204])
//!     .with_variant("style", "birch");
//! let bark = generate("log_side", &birch, 7, 16)?;
//!
//! // Mip levels down to one texel, filtered as the recipe's alpha asks for, and the colour
//! // of the surface from afar (linear RGB).
//! let alpha = recipe_info("log_side").expect("a recipe").alpha_mode();
//! assert_eq!(mip_chain(&bark, alpha).len(), 5);
//! let [r, g, b] = average_color(&bark);
//! assert!(r > 0.4 && g > 0.4 && b > 0.3);
//! # Ok::<(), cp_proctex::ProcTexError>(())
//! ```
//!
//! # What a texture is
//! [`generate`] paints a [`PbrTexture`]: albedo with alpha, a two-channel normal map,
//! roughness, emission and height, all of the same size. A texture is identified by
//! `(recipe, params, seed, size)` and is bit-identical for the same request on every machine
//! (all maths goes through `cp_core`'s deterministic functions, hashes and generator).
//!
//! Textures are authored on a 16×16 grid of *design units*; `size` only chooses how finely
//! that design is sampled. The native size is 16; larger powers of two give the same picture
//! with smoother outlines.
//!
//! # Guarantees
//! - **Seamless.** Every texture is one period of an image that repeats exactly on both axes;
//!   no operation knows where the tile's border is, and no pattern is anchored to it. Four
//!   textures put a joint of their own design on the border on purpose: the gaps between the
//!   boards of `planks`, the bark around `log_top`, the bevel of the `metal` plate, and
//!   `grass_side`, which repeats sideways only (its lower edge continues into `dirt` of the
//!   same seed).
//! - **Small palettes.** Four to six colours per texture, built as ramps from the material
//!   colour with cool shadows and warm highlights. Colours that callers pass in go through
//!   the same ramp builder, so they sit in the pack like the defaults.
//! - **Consistent channels.** Roughness and emission are properties of the palette entry a
//!   texel is painted with; the normal map is the slope of the height map; the height map
//!   follows the painted shapes.
//! - **The same surface at every size.** Heights are softened in design units, so a larger
//!   size shows the relief of the 16 px texture with the same slopes, not steeper ones.
//! - **Alpha as declared.** [`mip_chain`] filters alpha by an explicit [`AlphaMode`]: cutout
//!   textures keep their share of covered texels at every level, translucent ones their mean
//!   opacity.
//!
//! # Recipes and their parameters
//! Every recipe takes at least one colour, so a pack can reuse a pattern for another
//! material. [`recipe_info`] describes the parameters at run time; [`presets`] holds ready
//! parameter sets for the four tree species and four ores of the base game, and
//! [`contact_sheet`] shows all of it.
//!
//! | recipe | shows | parameters |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `stone` | bedrock in flat, layered blotches | `color` |
//! | `cobble` | rounded stones set in dark gaps | `color` |
//! | `gravel` | loose pebbles of mixed tones | `color` |
//! | `dirt` | crumbly soil with a few pebbles | `color` |
//! | `grass_top` | turf seen from above | `grass`, `style` (`turf`, `flowers`, `blossoms`, `litter`, `straw`) |
//! | `grass_side` | soil under a ragged grass fringe | `grass`, `dirt` |
//! | `sand` | wind ripples and single grains | `color` |
//! | `sandstone` | compacted sand in wavy strata | `color` |
//! | `snow` | soft drifts with blue shadows | `color` |
//! | `ice` | glossy, with glints and hairline cracks | `color` |
//! | `clay` | smooth, damp, in wide smears | `color` |
//! | `basalt` | dark honeycomb of column ends | `color` |
//! | `limestone` | pale and pitted | `color` |
//! | `slate` | thin horizontal sheets with ledges | `color` |
//! | `log_side` | bark | `bark`, `marks`, `style` (`furrowed`, `birch`, `scaly`, `smooth`) |
//! | `log_top` | growth rings inside a ring of bark | `bark`, `wood` |
//! | `planks` | four boards with butt joints | `wood` |
//! | `leaves` | foliage with gaps (cutout) | `leaf`, `blossom`, `style` (`broad`, `needle`) |
//! | `water` | a slow swell with glints | `color` |
//! | `lava` | glowing flows between dark crust (emissive) | `color` |
//! | `ore` | pieces of ore in stone | `ore`, `accent`, `rock`, `style` (`nuggets`, `lumps`, `flecks`, `crystals`) |
//! | `metal` | riveted plate with a bevelled edge | `color` |
//!
//! # Tinted recipes
//! Four recipes are meant to be multiplied by a biome tint and are therefore authored in
//! near-neutral, light tones (see [`RecipeInfo::tint`], [`Tint`] and [`tint_weight`]):
//!
//! | recipe | tint class | neutral part | parameter that bakes a colour in instead |
//! |---|---|---|---|
//! | `grass_top` | grass | everything | `grass` |
//! | `grass_side` | grass | the fringe; the soil keeps its colour | `grass` |
//! | `leaves` | foliage | everything | `leaf` |
//! | `water` | water | everything | `color` |
//!
//! A near-neutral colour parameter (the birch and spruce leaf presets) stays fully tintable
//! and shifts the result a little: lighter, darker, warmer or cooler than the biome's tint.
//! All other recipes carry their final colours.
//!
//! # Reviewing the art
//! `cargo test -p cp-proctex` writes the contact sheet to `target/proctex/contact_sheet.png`
//! and, under `target/proctex/review/`, pages with every texture large and tiled, its
//! channels with a lit preview, other seeds, and the numbers the tests check.

